#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693906 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\int 4 x\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int 4 x\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4 x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =4 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{cos}(x) \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =4 x\\left(\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\right)4\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =4 x\\operatorname{sin}(x)-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4 x\\operatorname{sin}(x)-4(-\\operatorname{cos}(x))+C \\\\  & =4 x\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693917 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\int x\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int x\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =1 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{sin}(x) \\\\ v & =-\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =x\\left(-\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\right)1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-x\\operatorname{cos}(x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}(x)+(\\operatorname{sin}(x))+C \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693918 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\int 6 x\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int 6 x\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =6 x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =6 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{sin}(x) \\\\ v & =-\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =6 x\\left(-\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\right)6\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-6 x\\operatorname{cos}(x)+6\\int \\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6 x\\operatorname{cos}(x)+6(\\operatorname{sin}(x))+C \\\\  & =-6 x\\operatorname{cos}(x)+6\\operatorname{sin}(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693922 :question/problem "$$\n\\int 9 x\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int 9 x\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =9 x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =9 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{sin}(2 x) \\\\ v & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =9 x\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)}{2}\\right)9\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{9 x\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)}{2}+\\frac{9\\int \\operatorname{cos}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9 x\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)}{2}+\\frac{9\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{2}\\right)}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{9 x\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)}{2}+\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693940 :question/problem "$$\n\\int 8 x\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int 8 x\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =8 x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =8 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{cos}(2 x) \\\\ v & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =8 x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{2}\\right)8\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{8 x\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{2}-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}(2 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8 x\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{2}-4\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)}{2}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{8 x\\operatorname{sin}(2 x)}{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}(2 x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693948 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\int x\\operatorname{sec}^{2}(x - 3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int x\\operatorname{sec}^{2}(x - 3)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =1 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}(x - 3) \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =x\\left(\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)\\right)1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $t=\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)$ and $\\mathrm{d}t=-\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)+\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)+\\ln|t|+C \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)+\\ln|\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis holds on each interval where $\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693957 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\int 2 x\\operatorname{csc}^{2}(x - 1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int 2 x\\operatorname{csc}^{2}(x - 1)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2 x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =2 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{csc}^{2}(x - 1) \\\\ v & =-\\operatorname{cot}(x - 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =2 x\\left(-\\operatorname{cot}(x - 1)\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\operatorname{cot}(x - 1)\\right)2\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $t=\\operatorname{sin}(x - 1)$ and $\\mathrm{d}t=\\operatorname{cos}(x - 1)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 1)+2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x - 1)}{\\operatorname{sin}(x - 1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 1)+2\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 1)+2\\ln|t|+C \\\\  & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 1)+2\\ln|\\operatorname{sin}(x - 1)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis holds on each interval where $\\operatorname{sin}(x - 1)\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693958 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\int 3 x\\operatorname{sec}^{2}(x - 3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int 3 x\\operatorname{sec}^{2}(x - 3)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3 x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =3 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{sec}^{2}(x - 3) \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =3 x\\left(\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)\\right)3\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $t=\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)$ and $\\mathrm{d}t=-\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =3 x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)-3\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3 x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)+3\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3 x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)+3\\ln|t|+C \\\\  & =3 x\\operatorname{tan}(x - 3)+3\\ln|\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis holds on each interval where $\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693966 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\int 2 x\\operatorname{csc}^{2}(x - 3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $I=\\int 2 x\\operatorname{csc}^{2}(x - 3)\\,\\text{d}x$. Use integration by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=uv-\\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nChoose the linear factor as $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2 x \\\\ u^{\\prime} & =2 \\\\ v^{\\prime} & =\\operatorname{csc}^{2}(x - 3) \\\\ v & =-\\operatorname{cot}(x - 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =2 x\\left(-\\operatorname{cot}(x - 3)\\right) \\\\ \\int vu^{\\prime}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(-\\operatorname{cot}(x - 3)\\right)2\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $t=\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)$ and $\\mathrm{d}t=\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 3)+2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x - 3)}{\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 3)+2\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 3)+2\\ln|t|+C \\\\  & =-2 x\\operatorname{cot}(x - 3)+2\\ln|\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis holds on each interval where $\\operatorname{sin}(x - 3)\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694030 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} - 2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- 2 x, & u^\\prime&=-2,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}, & v&=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[- x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}+\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)&=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}=- \\frac{\\pi}{4},\\\\\nF\\left(0\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{2}=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694031 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} x e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x, & u^\\prime&=1,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=e^{x}, & v&=e^{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[x e^{x}\\right]_{0}^{2}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(e^{x}\\right)\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[x e^{x}\\right]_{0}^{2}-\\int_{0}^{2}e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[x e^{x} - e^{x}\\right]_{0}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(2\\right)&=e^{2}=e^{2},\\\\\nF\\left(0\\right)&=-1=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(e^{2}\\right)-\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=1 + e^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694032 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{2}{3}}^{0} 3 x e^{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=3 x, & u^\\prime&=3,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=e^{3 x}, & v&=\\frac{e^{3 x}}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[x e^{3 x}\\right]_{- \\frac{2}{3}}^{0}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{- \\frac{2}{3}}^{0}\\left(\\frac{e^{3 x}}{3}\\right)\\left(3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[x e^{3 x}\\right]_{- \\frac{2}{3}}^{0}-\\int_{- \\frac{2}{3}}^{0}e^{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[x e^{3 x} - \\frac{e^{3 x}}{3}\\right]_{- \\frac{2}{3}}^{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(0\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{3}=- \\frac{1}{3},\\\\\nF\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{e^{2}}=- \\frac{1}{e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{e^{2}}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{3} + e^{-2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694033 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} 2 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x, & u^\\prime&=2,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}, & v&=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{2 x \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{2 x \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{2 x \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=- \\frac{2}{9}=- \\frac{2}{9},\\\\\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\pi}{9}=\\frac{\\pi}{9}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{2}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\pi}{9} - \\frac{2}{9}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694040 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- 2 x, & u^\\prime&=-2,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, & v&=- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi}-\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\pi\\right)&=\\pi=\\pi,\\\\\nF\\left(0\\right)&=0=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(\\pi\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694056 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} - 3 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- 3 x, & u^\\prime&=-3,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, & v&=- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{3 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\left(-3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{3 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{4},\\\\\nF\\left(0\\right)&=0=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694066 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} 2 x e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x, & u^\\prime&=2,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=e^{x}, & v&=e^{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[2 x e^{x}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(e^{x}\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[2 x e^{x}\\right]_{0}^{1}-2\\int_{0}^{1}e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[2 x e^{x} - 2 e^{x}\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(1\\right)&=0=0,\\\\\nF\\left(0\\right)&=-2=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694070 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x, & u^\\prime&=1,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, & v&=- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- \\frac{x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- \\frac{x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4},\\\\\nF\\left(0\\right)&=0=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694072 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} 3 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=3 x, & u^\\prime&=3,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}, & v&=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[3 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\left(3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[3 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}-3\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[3 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2},\\\\\nF\\left(0\\right)&=3=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(3\\right)\\\\\n&=-3 + \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694075 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{1}{3}}^{\\frac{2}{3}} - 2 x e^{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- 2 x, & u^\\prime&=-2,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=e^{3 x}, & v&=\\frac{e^{3 x}}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- \\frac{2 x e^{3 x}}{3}\\right]_{- \\frac{1}{3}}^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{- \\frac{1}{3}}^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\left(\\frac{e^{3 x}}{3}\\right)\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{2 x e^{3 x}}{3}\\right]_{- \\frac{1}{3}}^{\\frac{2}{3}}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{- \\frac{1}{3}}^{\\frac{2}{3}}e^{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- \\frac{2 x e^{3 x}}{3} + \\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{9}\\right]_{- \\frac{1}{3}}^{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)&=- \\frac{2 e^{2}}{9}=- \\frac{2 e^{2}}{9},\\\\\nF\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{4}{9 e}=\\frac{4}{9 e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{2 e^{2}}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{4}{9 e}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 \\left(- e^{3} - 2\\right)}{9 e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694077 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{- \\frac{1}{2}} 3 x e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=3 x, & u^\\prime&=3,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=e^{2 x}, & v&=\\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{3 x e^{2 x}}{2}\\right]_{-1}^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{-1}^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\left(\\frac{e^{2 x}}{2}\\right)\\left(3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 x e^{2 x}}{2}\\right]_{-1}^{- \\frac{1}{2}}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{-1}^{- \\frac{1}{2}}e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{3 x e^{2 x}}{2} - \\frac{3 e^{2 x}}{4}\\right]_{-1}^{- \\frac{1}{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)&=- \\frac{3}{2 e}=- \\frac{3}{2 e},\\\\\nF\\left(-1\\right)&=- \\frac{9}{4 e^{2}}=- \\frac{9}{4 e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{3}{2 e}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{9}{4 e^{2}}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\left(3 - 2 e\\right)}{4 e^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694080 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} - 3 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- 3 x, & u^\\prime&=-3,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}, & v&=\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- \\frac{3 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\left(-3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{3 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}+\\frac{3}{2}\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[- \\frac{3 x \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{3 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{8}=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{8},\\\\\nF\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi}{8}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{8} - \\frac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694081 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose the factors so that differentiating the linear factor simplifies the remaining integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- 2 x, & u^\\prime&=-2,\\\\\nv^\\prime&=\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}, & v&=- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu^\\prime\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{2 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{2 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function and combine the two primitive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left[\\frac{2 x \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3} - \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)$ denote the primitive inside the brackets. Evaluate each endpoint, then subtract the entire lower value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=- \\frac{2}{9}=- \\frac{2}{9},\\\\\nF\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{2 \\pi}{9}=\\frac{2 \\pi}{9}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\left(- \\frac{2}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{9}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{2 \\pi}{9} - \\frac{2}{9}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694089 :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$$\n\\int 6 xe^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 6 xe^{- x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =6 x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′} & =e^{- x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=- e^{- x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6 x\\cdot e^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6 x\\cdot (- e^{- x})-\\int (- e^{- x})\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6 xe^{- x}+6\\cdot \\int e^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6 xe^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-6 xe^{- x}+6\\cdot \\int e^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6 xe^{- x}-6\\cdot e^{- x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollecting the linear terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6 xe^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =-6 x e^{- x} - 6 e^{- x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694090 :question/problem "$$\n\\int x3^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x3^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′} & =3^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{3^{x}}{\\ln{(3 )}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot 3^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{3^{x}}{\\ln{(3 )}}-\\int \\frac{3^{x}}{\\ln{(3 )}}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x3^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3^{x} x}{\\ln{(3 )}}-\\int \\frac{3^{x}}{\\ln{(3 )}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\ln{(3 )}}\\cdot x3^{x}-\\frac{1}{\\ln{(3 )}^{2}}\\cdot 3^{x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3^{x}}{\\ln{(3 )}}(x - \\frac{1}{\\ln{(3 )}})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollecting the linear terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x3^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\ln{(3 )}} - \\frac{1}{\\ln{(3 )}^{2}}\\right)3^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694098 :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$$\n\\int x4^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x4^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′} & =4^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{4^{x}}{\\ln{(4 )}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot 4^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{4^{x}}{\\ln{(4 )}}-\\int \\frac{4^{x}}{\\ln{(4 )}}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x4^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4^{x} x}{\\ln{(4 )}}-\\int \\frac{4^{x}}{\\ln{(4 )}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\ln{(4 )}}\\cdot x4^{x}-\\frac{1}{\\ln{(4 )}^{2}}\\cdot 4^{x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4^{x}}{\\ln{(4 )}}(x - \\frac{1}{\\ln{(4 )}})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollecting the linear terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x4^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{x}{\\ln{(4 )}} - \\frac{1}{\\ln{(4 )}^{2}}\\right)4^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694100 :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int 2 xe^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 2 xe^{- x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2 x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′} & =e^{- x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=- e^{- x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2 x\\cdot e^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2 x\\cdot (- e^{- x})-\\int (- e^{- x})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2 xe^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 xe^{- x}+2\\cdot \\int e^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2 xe^{- x}-2\\cdot e^{- x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollecting the linear terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2 xe^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =-2 x e^{- x} - 2 e^{- x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694108 :question/problem "$$\n\\int 6 xe^{7 x - 4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 6 xe^{7 x - 4}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =6 x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′} & =e^{7 x - 4}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{e^{7 x - 4}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6 x\\cdot e^{7 x - 4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6 x\\cdot \\frac{e^{7 x - 4}}{7}-\\int \\frac{e^{7 x - 4}}{7}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6 xe^{7 x - 4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 6 xe^{7 x - 4}-\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\int e^{7 x - 4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 6 xe^{7 x - 4}-\\frac{6}{49}\\cdot e^{7 x - 4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollecting the linear terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6 xe^{7 x - 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =\\frac{6 x e^{7 x - 4}}{7} - \\frac{6 e^{7 x - 4}}{49}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186694125 :question/problem "$$\n\\int 8 xe^{6 x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 8 xe^{6 x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =8 x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=8 \\\\ {v}^{′} & =e^{6 x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{e^{6 x}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 8 x\\cdot e^{6 x}\\,\\text{d}x & =8 x\\cdot \\frac{e^{6 x}}{6}-\\int \\frac{e^{6 x}}{6}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8 xe^{6 x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 8 xe^{6 x}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\int e^{6 x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 8 xe^{6 x}-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot e^{6 x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollecting the linear terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8 xe^{6 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =\\frac{4 x e^{6 x}}{3} - \\frac{2 e^{6 x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696267 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x+2y}{x-y+1}$." :question/worked-solution "The numerator is a polynomial, so it imposes no restrictions. The function is well-defined precisely when its denominator is nonzero:\n\n$$\nx-y+1\\ne 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ne x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ne x+1\\}.\n$$\n\nPoints on either side of the line are allowed. Only the line $y=x+1$ is excluded."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696271 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y+1}}{\\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}-1}$." :question/worked-solution "Three conditions must hold:\n\n- The numerator's radicand is nonnegative: $y+1\\ge 0$.\n- The denominator's radicand is nonnegative: $4-x^2-y^2\\ge 0$.\n- The whole denominator is nonzero: $\\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}-1\\ne 0$.\n\nThe first two conditions give $y\\ge -1$ and $x^2+y^2\\le 4$. To find the excluded points within this region, solve the denominator-zero equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}-1&=0\\\\\n\\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}&=1\\\\\n4-x^2-y^2&=1\\\\\nx^2+y^2&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring introduces no extra solutions here: both sides of the square-root equation are nonnegative. Therefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ge -1,\\ x^2+y^2\\le 4,\\ x^2+y^2\\ne 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThe outer boundary $x^2+y^2=4$ is allowed wherever $y\\ge -1$: there the denominator is $-1$, not zero. The excluded circle has $x^2+y^2=3$, and only its portion in the allowed region matters. For example, $(2,0)$ and $(0,-1)$ are in the domain, while $(\\sqrt{3},0)$ is excluded."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696286 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y+2}}{x+y-1}$." :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which both conditions hold:\n\n- The expression under the square root is nonnegative.\n- The denominator is nonzero.\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+2&\\ge 0 &&\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ge -2,\\\\\nx+y-1&\\ne 0 &&\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ne 1-x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ge -2,\\ y\\ne 1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nA zero numerator is allowed, so the boundary $y=-2$ is included except at $(3,-2)$, where the denominator is zero. The denominator may be positive or negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696514 :question/problem "The real-valued function $f(x,y)$ is defined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{\\log_2(xy^2)+\\sqrt{x-y}}{2^{-y}-x}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(2,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ throughout the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2,-2) &= \\frac{\\log_2\\bigl(2(-2)^2\\bigr)+\\sqrt{2-(-2)}}{2^{-(-2)}-2} \\\\\n        &= \\frac{\\log_2(8)+\\sqrt{4}}{2^2-2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe logarithm has positive argument $8$, the square root has nonnegative argument $4$, and the denominator is $4-2=2$, which is nonzero. Thus the expression is defined over the real numbers. Since $2^3=8$, we have $\\log_2(8)=3$, and the principal square root of $4$ is $2$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2,-2) &= \\frac{3+2}{4-2} \\\\\n        &= \\frac{5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696521 :question/problem "Evaluate the real-valued function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2y-x}{x^3-y^2-7}\n$$\n\nat the point $(2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=2$ and $y=-1$ into both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y-x &= 2(-1)-2 = -4, \\\\\nx^3-y^2-7 &= 2^3-(-1)^2-7 \\\\\n            &= 8-1-7 = 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis would require division by zero, so $f(2,-1)$ is undefined. The point $(2,-1)$ is outside the domain; the nonzero numerator does not change that."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696526 :question/problem "Evaluate $z=x^2+xy-5$ at the point $(-2,3)$." :question/worked-solution "The first coordinate gives $x=-2$ and the second gives $y=3$. Substitute both values, keeping parentheses around the negative input:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (-2)^2+(-2)(3)-5 \\\\\n  &= 4-6-5 \\\\\n  &= -7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the function at $(-2,3)$ is $-7$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696533 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n2\\cos^3{x}+2\\cos{x}\\sin^2{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Factor the common factor $2\\cos{x}$ from both terms:\n\n$$\n2\\cos^3{x}+2\\cos{x}\\sin^2{x}\n=2\\cos{x}(\\cos^2{x}+\\sin^2{x}).\n$$\n\nThe Pythagorean identity gives $\\cos^2{x}+\\sin^2{x}=1$, so\n\n$$\n2\\cos{x}(\\cos^2{x}+\\sin^2{x})=2\\cos{x}\\cdot 1=2\\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $2\\cos{x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696534 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n\\sin^4{x}-\\sin{x}\\cos^3{x}+\\sin^2{x}\\cos^2{x}-\\sin^3{x}\\cos{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Group the two positive terms and the two negative terms. This makes two different common factors available:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(\\sin^4{x}+\\sin^2{x}\\cos^2{x})\\\\\n&\\qquad-(\\sin{x}\\cos^3{x}+\\sin^3{x}\\cos{x}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactor $\\sin^2{x}$ from the first group and $\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$ from the group being subtracted:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\sin^2{x}(\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x})\\\\\n&\\qquad-\\sin{x}\\cos{x}(\\cos^2{x}+\\sin^2{x}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach parenthesized sum equals $1$ by the Pythagorean identity. The minus sign applies to the whole second group:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^2{x}\\cdot 1-\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\\cdot 1\n&=\\sin^2{x}-\\sin{x}\\cos{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $\\sin^2{x}-\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696546 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n\\sin^3{x}-\\cos{x}+\\sin{x}\\cos^2{x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first and third terms have a common factor $\\sin{x}$. Group them, keeping the middle term negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(\\sin^3{x}+\\sin{x}\\cos^2{x})-\\cos{x}\\\\\n&=\\sin{x}(\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x})-\\cos{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x}=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\sin{x}(\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x})-\\cos{x}\\\\\n&=\\sin{x}\\cdot 1-\\cos{x}\\\\\n&=\\sin{x}-\\cos{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe simplified expression is $\\sin{x}-\\cos{x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696633 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y}}{2y+x-4}$." :question/worked-solution "The numerator radicand must be nonnegative, and the polynomial denominator must be nonzero. A zero numerator is allowed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ge0\\\\\n2y+x-4&\\ne 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 2y+x\\ne4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ge0,\\ 2y+x\\ne4\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696635 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y-x^2+1}}{\\sqrt{1-\\left(2x^3-y\\right)}}$." :question/worked-solution "The numerator radicand may equal zero. The denominator radicand must be strictly positive, since its square root is the complete denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-x^2+1&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y-x^2\\ge-1\\\\\n1-\\left(2x^3-y\\right)&>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 2x^3-y<1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y-x^2\\ge-1,\\ 2x^3-y<1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186696642 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{-1-\\left(y-x\\right)}}{\\sqrt{2y^3-x-1}}$." :question/worked-solution "The numerator radicand may equal zero. The denominator radicand must be strictly positive, since its square root is the complete denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-1-\\left(y-x\\right)&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y-x\\le-1\\\\\n2y^3-x-1&>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 2y^3-x>1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y-x\\le-1,\\ 2y^3-x>1\\}.\n$$"}]}
